the a45 is one of the busiest highways  in Germany it's an important north-south  connection and a transit route for  truckers it's also one of the highways  with the most bridges like the Lenna  Valley Bridge seen here more than 80,000  vehicles cross it each day far too many  the bridge which was built in the 1960s  is showing signs of wear and tear caused  mainly by heavy transport it's already  been closed to trucks weighing more than  40 tons  we have cracks in the underside of the  bridge which have now been filled in  though the bridge design was too narrow  the components are exposed to a lot of  dynamic strain and that causes new  cracks we have to constantly keep track  of it with this bridge to make sure the  new cracks get filled in quickly the  companies in the area are dependent on  the highway the ironworks in anta book  is having problems delivering to  customers because of the bridges weight  limit this iron pipe intended for a wind  turbine weighs some 30 tons it has to be  transported from north rhine-westphalia  to the harbour in Antwerp Belgium by a  special transporter and both together  are too heavy for the ailing bridge the  head of the manufacturer is furious we  have a transport that weighs about 60  tons in total including the vehicle and  we're not allowed to take it on the 8:45  that makes me very angry because then we  have longer shipping times that affects  the drivers rest breaks in some cases we  don't get to the port in time the number  of vehicles on Germany's highways has  tripled in the past ten years that has  consequences there are plans to replace  the Lenna Valley Bridge Construction is  scheduled to start in August other  bridges will be refurbished but there  isn't enough money to renovate the  entire a45 in our state highway  authority we're looking at as many as  800 bridges and more keep being added  that need to be rebuilt if we add them  all up we wouldn't need billions and we  don't have that much money right now we  have to arrange funding for the future  there are also plans to widen the road  from four and five lanes to a total of  six lanes but that will take years  despite the transport difficulties the  ironworks intends to keep its  manufacturing facilities here  it makes parts for gas and oil pipelines  as well as offshore wind parks more than  80 percent gets shipped abroad isn't  yeah we started here as a private  company to help contribute to the  economic security of the region and  create jobs we want to do what we can  we've invested millions but we're  hindered by the fact that we can't use  these roads it's some 250 kilometers to  Antwerp and the special transporter has  to take a big detour to get there these  kinds of deliveries won't be able to use  the a45 for another ten years  that's how long the reconstruction work  is expected to take
 

